[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
SysManage server is installed but NOT configured.  Five steps, in order.

Architecture: nginx is the front door and terminates TLS.  The application
itself runs behind it on 127.0.0.1:8080 and is not reachable from the network
-- uvicorn is an implementation detail, not a public listener.  Do not expose
port 8080.

1. PostgreSQL
   ----------
     service postgresql initdb          # first time only
     sysrc postgresql_enable=YES
     service postgresql start
     su -m postgres -c 'createuser sysmanage'
     su -m postgres -c 'createdb sysmanage -O sysmanage'
     su -m postgres -c "psql -c \"ALTER USER sysmanage PASSWORD 'secret';\""

2. Configuration -- %%PREFIX%%/etc/sysmanage.yaml
   ---------------------------------------------
   Set the database credentials from step 1, then under "security:":

     admin_userid MUST BE AN EMAIL ADDRESS.  The login endpoint validates it
     as one, so the shipped default of "admin" is rejected with HTTP 422
     before the password is ever checked.  Use e.g. admin@example.com.

   Replace password_salt and jwt_secret -- both ship as CHANGE_ME
   placeholders.  The file is mode 0640, group sysmanage, because it holds
   the database password; keep it that way.

   That bootstrap login is a recovery credential, not an account.  Use it
   once, create a real administrator in the UI, and the interface will keep
   warning you until you do.

3. Database schema
   ---------------
     su -m sysmanage -c '%%PYTHON_CMD%% \
       %%PREFIX%%/libexec/sysmanage/scripts/sysmanage_migrate.py --status'
     su -m sysmanage -c '%%PYTHON_CMD%% \
       %%PREFIX%%/libexec/sysmanage/scripts/sysmanage_migrate.py'

4. TLS + nginx
   -----------
   Install a certificate and key -- this port does not generate them, because
   a certificate a package manufactured for you is one no client should
   trust.  Use security/acme.sh, security/py-certbot, or your own CA:

     %%PREFIX%%/etc/sysmanage/tls/server.crt
     %%PREFIX%%/etc/sysmanage/tls/server.key

   Then include the shipped config and start nginx:

     cp %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/sysmanage-nginx.conf.sample \
        %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/sysmanage-nginx.conf
     # add to the http { } block of %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:
     #     include %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/sysmanage-nginx.conf;
     nginx -t
     sysrc nginx_enable=YES
     service nginx start

   nginx will refuse to start until the certificate exists.  That is
   deliberate: a management console must not come up in cleartext because a
   file was missing.

5. Start the server
   ----------------
     sysrc sysmanage_enable=YES
     service sysmanage start

   Then browse to https://<this-host>/

The service runs as the unprivileged "sysmanage" user, generates its agent
mTLS CA into /var/db/sysmanage/certs on first start (separate from the web
certificate above), and logs to /var/log/sysmanage.  That account is
deliberately NOT the one used by sysutils/sysmanage-agent, which holds
root-equivalent sudo grants.
EOM
}
]
